+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See `proposal.md - Why`. The service is TypeScript on Node 24 (built-in `node:sqlite`),
+with a strict layering already in place: routes parse and delegate, services hold
+business rules, repositories own all SQL. Time comes from an injected `Clock`. Errors
+are thrown as `AppError(status, code, message)` and the router maps them to
+`{ error: { code, message } }`.
+
+`OrderService` currently receives only `OrderRepository` and the clock;
+`CouponService` owns coupon administration. The schema already contains every column
+this feature needs (`orders.discount_cents`, `orders.final_amount_cents`,
+`orders.coupon_code`, `coupons.used_count`, `coupon_redemptions`), so no migration is
+required.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Apply a coupon to a pending order through one new endpoint, following the existing
+  route → service → repository layering.
+- Make eligibility, discount arithmetic, and persistence deterministic and testable
+  with the injected clock.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Removing/reversing an applied coupon, or re-pricing after `pay`/`cancel`.
+- Changing the coupon admin API or the DB schema.
+- Concurrency hardening beyond the single-process, synchronous `node:sqlite` model.
+
+## Decisions
+
+**Coupon eligibility lives in `OrderService`, not `CouponService`.**
+Applying a coupon is a property of an order transition, so the orchestration belongs
+with the order rules; `OrderService` gains a `CouponRepository` and a redemption
+persistence port. Alternative: put it in `CouponService` — rejected because it would
+need to reach into orders and blur the ownership of the order update.
+
+**Validation order is fail-fast and matches the spec scenarios.**
+Missing code → coupon lookup → `ACTIVE` → within `[validFrom, validUntil]` → order
+status `PENDING` and no existing coupon → `amountCents >= minAmountCents` → usage
+limit. Each failure throws a distinct `AppError` with status 400 (409 for order-state
+conflicts, 404 for an unknown order).
+
+**Discount arithmetic is integer-only.**
+`FIXED`: `discount = value`. `PERCENT`: `discount = Math.floor(amountCents * value /
+100)`. Then `discount = Math.min(discount, maxDiscountCents ?? discount)` and
+`discount = Math.min(discount, amountCents)` so `finalAmountCents` is never negative.
+Percentages round down so the customer is never over-discounted relative to the exact
+value. Alternative: `Math.round` — rejected as less predictable and harder to assert.
+
+**Usage tracking uses the existing tables.**
+On success, insert a `coupon_redemptions` row and increment
+`coupons.used_count`; the usage-limit check reads the persisted `usedCount`. This keeps
+the audit trail the schema was designed for. A new `CouponRedemptionRepository` (or
+methods on `CouponRepository`) owns this SQL, keeping SQL out of the service.
+
+**Redemption is applied atomically at the DB level.**
+`node:sqlite` is synchronous, so the update of the order, the increment of `used_count`,
+and the insert of the redemption run in one service call guarded by a
+`db.exec("BEGIN")`/`COMMIT` transaction exposed through the repository, so a mid-way
+failure cannot leave an order discounted without a redemption record.
+
+**Assumptions recorded for unspecified details.**
+The task rules do not state how `max_discount_cents`, `usage_limit`, order status, or
+re-application behave; the schema implies them. We choose: honor `maxDiscountCents`
+when set (NULL = no cap), enforce `usageLimit` when set (NULL = unlimited), only allow
+`PENDING` orders that have no coupon yet, and reject an unknown order with 404. These
+are captured as spec requirements so they are explicit and testable.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Hidden coupling: a caller may expect 404 for a bad coupon] → The task's acceptance
+  criteria explicitly say an invalid coupon returns 400, so all coupon-validation
+  failures use 400; only a missing order uses 404.
+- [Percent rounding disagreements with an external expectation] → Documented and
+  covered by an explicit rounding scenario; floor is the conservative choice.
+- [Extra strictness (PENDING-only, no re-application) could reject a legitimate retry]
+  → These are isolated in the service and easy to relax if a requirement says
+  otherwise; they do not affect the happy path.
+- [Transaction handling around `used_count`] → Single-process synchronous driver; a
+  simple `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK` wrapper is sufficient.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+None — the remaining details are recorded as assumptions above and encoded in the
+specs.
